1. **Inspection, with runs.** `clerk.py test` passes. The eight solo-kernel tool tests passed (2026-10-15). `lcheck.py` on `00.00.00.kernel.draft`, default read-only run: reworked 0 error, 0 warn, 60 info; pending 24 error, 41 warn, 32 info (same on 2026-10-15 and 2026-10-17). Evidence and the reuse table: `plan.1`.
2. **A development plan for the app**, page `1008.app-plan` in the lib book: overview (face `04`), eight parts, four batches. The first version is the **batch desk**. Eight stages, each ending usable, with a demo, a gate and recovery (`plan.5`).
3. **28 lines answered by the Director**: `05.01` (13) and `05.02` (7) in full, and `05.03` #01 to #07 and #11 (8). Each reply is kept whole with a status after it.
4. **`AGENTS.003.md` drafted** at the repo root beside the seat, under a standing permission (`05.01` #11). It adds a header stamp, branch commits on a named branch only, the AGENTS-draft permission, and the `AGENTS.VVV.09.md` retire name. `AGENTS.md` was not touched.
5. **The first build assignment**, `plan.7` version 002: branch `build/2006.batch-desk`, read-only stage 1 (place, tree, fixture), its own batch `2006.batch-desk/05.01`. Version 001 is kept as `plan.7.first-assignment.001.09.md`. Not released.
6. **The desk command list, drafted** (`plan.8`): 23 commands from 122 batch files, each with what it asks, what follows, and its source; `>stop` added.
7. **A review of the solo-kernel sample** (the kernel redraft), filed twice: the return `colab/returns/claude-solo-kernel-sample-pass01-2026-10-15.md` and seven `>note` lines on `05.04`.

## What was learned about the clerk
1. **The desk is for two.** The Director directs, the prime reasons, the clerk (compute) does the mechanical part on call. A desk lends authority to shelves, where workers work inside books; a book is a world with its own rules and words. The design is fractal. (Director, `05.03` #04, #05.)
2. **The app is the desk a model sits at.** Every write goes through it. Compute fills headers, stamps, versions, addresses, links; the model writes what needs judgment: the why, the body, the ask (`05.01` #05; `2001.page.README`).
3. **A batch is the review queue.** All work, stops, conflicts and findings are `#NN` lines on a `05.NN` before or as the work happens, so no model work goes unseen. The `1006.redraft` page is the model: face `01` says what and why, each `05.NN` is a round, face `06` holds notes (`05.01` #08).
4. **The command is the kind.** The line shape is `1006`'s: `#NN >command subject`, issue when there is a conflict, recommend, `Ask: >command` with the reason, path, scale, reply, status. A desk command list governs; a command not on it is kicked back. `>note` is a finding, `>stop` an escalation, carry is `reason: From 05.NN #NN` with `Carried to 05.NN #NN` (`05.02` #02). A plain `kind:` field was proposed and dropped as too lean.
5. **The reply is the Director's, from the user side only.** In solo work a model skips the reply, works through the batch, and writes the status after; a blank reply with a status is the solo mark. The Director may edit `05.NN` directly (dev mode); replies written outside the app are listed and confirmed in bulk (`05.02` #03; `05.01` #06).
6. **Anything that violates the design is kicked back.** That keeps a model in a narrow band and lets it tell when something is wrong: writes outside a grant, unknown commands, a version that is not seat plus one, a reply from a model command (`05.03` #04).
7. **Files are the truth.** Any index or database is a rebuildable cache, until the Director decides otherwise by its own stage (`05.01` #02).
8. **Writes are create-only or append-only**, version-checked by a SHA-256 of the raw bytes, swapped in one step, journaled with a before-copy in `.librarian/` (`plan.4`).
9. **Rules are looked up**, book, then shelf, then desk, then kernel. Later the clerk reads the words of a prompt before the model does, finds key words and their brands in the dictionary and concordance, and hands the model the rules it needs, so a repo carries a business's own words (`05.03` #05).
10. **History lives in git branches.** Models commit on a named work branch, one commit per closed line; merging into `main` is the Director's (`05.01` #07; `05.03` #07).
11. **Provenance works at scale.** The redraft stamped 258 drafts with seat path, version and hash. The face engine should keep that block. The same review showed the main risk: a cleanup that drops face `06` history. Mark and retire; never drop (`05.02` #07; return hole 1).
12. **Links written by filename break.** 125 of 338 links in the sample pointed at missing files. The clerk writes links at seat time (`2003.reference`).
13. **One core, not twelve scripts.** Three copies of the address rules, hard-coded paths, and two face makers with opposite face `06` policy (`clerk.py`, solo `face.py`) are the reason for one Python core with no screen, commands over it, a Textual screen later, then a Qt window (`05.01` #03).
14. **For a cold model the cost is reading.** Rules point rather than restate, and the same fact can sit in several places and disagree (the solo return said the Director ruled one way on face 06; the `2002.face` 05.04 record said the other). The clerk should show one current answer per question, with its source.

